ON_ERRORS: [ErrorSpec; 15]` of `ErrorSpec { kind, meaning, remedy, repeats }` (`repeats` is not served), which `kind()`, `remedy()` and `Display` read; every message is a fixed string — the row's meaning, the `Io` arm appending the kind's name and the `NotSettled` arm the class's — and none carries a path, a label, an id or a value; `StateDirTooLong`'s message states its bound as a figure, at most 94 bytes as given on Linux and 90 on every other target, and its remedy is to name a shorter path for the state directory, or a relative one, which counts only its own bytes; two rows share a remedy, each later row naming the earlier — `dead` repeats `no-session`'s, `answer-too-large` repeats `not-settled`'s (packages/escher-driver/src/error.rs:12-224; packages/escher-driver/src/error.rs:61-69; packages/escher-driver/src/error.rs:79-177). The wire is crate-private and versioned `v3`: one request line and one reply line per connection, `\n`-terminated — requests `hello v3`, `stop v3` and `call v3 <verb> [<name>=<kind>:<value>]…` (kinds `t` text · `n` whole number · `f` flag; the verb, the names and a text value percent-escaped byte for byte — every byte but an ASCII letter, a digit, `-`, `_`, `.` and `~` — and decoded strictly: uppercase hex only, no escape of a byte that needs none, the decoded bytes UTF-8, a number in its one spelling), at most 16,384 bytes where `v1` admitted 64, the widest call the schema admits being 15,360 bytes of values; replies `ok v3 pid={pid} label={label} served={n} idle_expiry_s={s}`, `ok v3 stopping`, `refused version` (a known word with another version) and `refused malformed` (anything else, a call the decoder does not read included), at most 128 bytes — the widest `hello` measures 122 — and, to a call, `ok v3 accepted <json>`, `ok v3 refused <json>`, `ok v3 oversize` or `ok v3 recording-failed` — sent in place of the answer when the host's recording ended after the call, the client reading it as `RecordingFailed` — an answer bounded at 1,04